# MailPilot Development Rules

## Tech Stack & Architecture
- Framework: Next.js 15 (App Router, Server Actions, Route Handlers).
- Runtime: Node.js on Vercel Serverless (10s max timeout on Hobby). Do NOT use long-polling daemons or background loops.
- Database: PostgreSQL (Neon/Supabase) via Prisma ORM.
- Styling: Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui components, Lucide icons.
- APIs: Official `googleapis` npm package, OpenRouter REST API, Local Ollama.

## Critical Patterns & Constraints
- Extensible Settings: Never add static columns to `AccountSettings` for minor toggles. Store all automation toggles in the `rules Json` field validated with Zod (`accountRulesSchema`).
- Serverless Execution: When handling Pub/Sub webhooks (`/api/webhooks/gmail`), return `200 OK` immediately and process background work using Next.js 15 `after()`.
- API Scopes: Google OAuth must request `openid`, `userinfo.email`, `userinfo.profile`, `gmail.modify`, and `gmail.compose`, always with `access_type: "offline"` and `prompt: "consent"`.
- TypeScript: Strict typing everywhere. No `any` types. Validate incoming payloads with Zod.
- UI: Design mobile-first with desktop density. Ensure all data tables collapse cleanly to card feeds on screens < 768px.

## Zero Hardcoding
- NEVER hardcode or introduce candidate-specific, user-specific, or profession-specific values (e.g., specific names, usernames, role titles, companies, schools, or hardcoded title regexes) into application logic, prompts, schemas, or sanitization steps; all behavior must remain 100% dynamic, multi-tenant, and universal across any industry, discipline, or career level to be usable by anyone.